#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0395970e-0d9a-5762-bb5c-e46f2d38b8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5z+11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423949} {:answer/id #uuid "4b65c837-ece2-5b2d-a1d6-63cd8cc028b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423953} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5c8545-4573-5ed8-bdbb-b47cb8a05b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423954} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e87eac-968b-5331-95b6-4aa94e8c328b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423956} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a52ec1-f084-5a8c-880c-5ed843166cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a52ec1-f084-5a8c-880c-5ed843166cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423958} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc839475-9e1b-518e-9233-6715536351ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3a2e450-d136-5240-a920-568065ef68bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-33146" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,2,1⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨-2,1,5⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨-2,1,5⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,1,5⟩ & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,1,5⟩ & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,5⟩ & =7 \\\\ -2x+y+5z & =7 \\\\ -2x+y+5z-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x-y-5z+7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13bd6234-56f6-541c-bfc5-f390a8cb5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨3,-2,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423950} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1ce09d-4b43-5ecf-b718-dbc33ca70dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,2,3⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423951} {:answer/id #uuid "2c57426d-3447-5790-bc09-7f27391e4e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,3⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423952} {:answer/id #uuid "95f99c20-a235-5660-8d6b-b04b727483d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423955} {:answer/id #uuid "b1292071-77e9-579a-8b2f-0a1e4da37f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,\\frac{1}{3}⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f99c20-a235-5660-8d6b-b04b727483d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423955} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa9de33-9d04-55cd-94a1-cecb483c4e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e005b4ab-002a-57d1-b9b7-3fc85eeb395f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70819" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $x-2y+3z=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z & =0 \\\\ 3z & =-x+2y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=0.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10191765-520e-51b1-b616-6aed299a1951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336181} {:answer/id #uuid "7daf1c63-6468-51ad-b441-f7fb571f725e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3z+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337169} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9bfc06-e53d-546a-8e5c-41d1b1999a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337685} {:answer/id #uuid "c52efad2-ab20-5349-b4af-05ecdf7d704d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337785} {:answer/id #uuid "f09bbdc2-0929-5b68-b7a0-f2e4bd00ad0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09bbdc2-0929-5b68-b7a0-f2e4bd00ad0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338137} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f92cb71-8832-51de-ba25-618de4d73dc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee138f11-2a9e-5208-a49a-f5a947f6c08b" :question/math-academy-id "q-70833" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,0,-3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,2,0⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
